<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-function-notation-evaluating-functions" xmlns:xml="http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace">
  <title>Function Notation and Evaluating Functions</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>A function assigns exactly one output to each input. Function notation provides a convenient way to describe this input-output relationship. Before evaluating functions, it is important to understand what the inputs and outputs represent in a real-world situation.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-understanding-function-notation">
    <title>Understanding Function Notation</title>
    <p>
      We not only use function notation to denote the function itself, <m>y=f(x)</m>, but we also use it to represent specific values that a function takes. For example, we may write <m>3=f(1)</m> or <m>f(1)=3</m> to mean at the input of <m>1</m> the function <m>f</m> gives the output of <m>3</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each of the following are equivalent ways to represent the input-output pair associated with a function <m>g</m> sending an input of <m>3</m> to the output <m>9</m>:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><m>g(3)=9</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>9=g(3)</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>(3,9)</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>(3,g(3))</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>x=3</m> when <m>g(x)=9</m></p></li>
    </ul>

    <example xml:id="ex-bicycle-distance">
      <title>Distance Traveled</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Let <m>D=m(t)</m> represent the total distance traveled, in miles, after riding a bicycle for <m>t</m> minutes. Interpret the statement <m>m(45)=11</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>The input is <m>45</m> minutes and the output is <m>11</m> miles. After riding for 45 minutes, the cyclist has traveled 11 miles.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <remark>
      <p>Questions like "<m>f(3)=</m>___" begin with an input and ask for an output. Questions like "<m>f(x)=5</m> when <m>x=</m>___" begin with an output and ask for an input.</p>
    </remark>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Evaluate-Diagrams">
    <title>Evaluating with Diagrams</title>
      
      <p>
        In the previous section, <xref ref="subsec-Is-it-a-Function-Diagrams"/>, we learned that <xref ref="Func-Bubble-Diagram-figure1" text="type-global"/> is representing a function. It was not given a name previously, let's call it the function <m>f</m>. We have three input values, so we have three evaluations of this function. We start at each input value and follow the arrow to find the output of the function there.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="Func-Bubble-Diagram-figure1-revised">
        <title>Bubble Diagram</title>
        <caption>A bubble diagram of a function.</caption>
     <image width="60%">
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="Bubble-Diagram-prefigure1-revised">
        <title>Bubble Diagram</title>
        <description>Bubble diagram of a function.</description>
     <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
  <definition> top=1.75 </definition>
  <definition> mid=2 </definition>
  <definition> top=1.75 </definition>
  <definition> mid=2 </definition> 
  <coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
    <label anchor="(-mid,3.4)" alignment="center">
      Input
     </label>
    <ellipse center="(-mid,0)" axes="(1.5,3)" stroke="black"/>
    <label anchor="(mid,3.4)" alignment="center">
      Output
     </label>
    <ellipse center="(mid,0)" axes="(1.5,3)" stroke="black"/>
    <line endpoints="((-mid+.2,0), (mid-.6,top))" stroke="blue" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
    <line endpoints="((-mid+.2,top), (mid-.6,-top))" stroke="blue" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
    <line endpoints="((-mid+.2,-top), (mid-.5,0))" stroke="blue" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
    <label anchor="(mid-.4,top)" alignment="center">
      Apple
    </label>
    <label anchor="(mid-.4,0)" alignment="center">
      Pear
    </label>
    <label anchor="(mid-.4,-top-.1)" alignment="center">
      Grape
    </label>
    <label anchor="(-mid-.2,top)" alignment="center">
      A
    </label>
    <label anchor="(-mid-.2,0)" alignment="center">
      B
    </label>
    <label anchor="(-mid-.2,-top)" alignment="center">
      C
    </label>
  </coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure> 
 </image>
      </figure>

<p>
  Give it a try and check your work with the example below.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-evaluating-diagram1">
      <title>Evaluating a Function from a Diagram</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Using the bubble diagram, <xref ref="Func-Bubble-Diagram-figure1" text="type-global"/> identify the three input values and evaluate the function at each. Use <m>f</m> as the function name.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <ul>
      <li><p><m>f(A)=</m> Grape</p></li>
      <li><p><m>f(B)=</m> Apple</p></li>
      <li><p><m>f(C)=</m> Pear</p></li>
      </ul>
      </solution>
    </example>

</subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Evaluate-Tables">
    <title>Evaluating with Tables</title>
    <p>
      Recall that <m>f(x)</m> is the same as <m>y</m>. When looking at a table we need to identify which column/row has data related to the input variable and which has data related to the output variable. We do not always use <m>x</m> to represent input and <m>f(x)</m> for the output. Different problems may use a different input variable, and a different name for the function.
    </p>
    <p>
      Below is a table, <xref ref="table-evaluate1"/>, that represents a function. Remember, we know it's a function as each input has a unique output associated with it. For a table, this means that each input value appears only once in the table. The first row represents the input values, and the second row represents the output values. The function is named <m>f</m>, and the input variable is <m>x</m>.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table-evaluate1">
        <title>The Function <m>f</m></title>
          <tabular>
            <col right="minor"/>
            <col right="minor"/>
            <col right="minor"/>
            <col right="minor"/>
            <col/>
            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                <cell><m>9</m></cell>
                <cell><m>-3</m></cell>
                <cell><m>40</m></cell>
            </row>

            <row>
                <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
                <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                <cell><m>-6</m></cell>
                <cell><m>9</m></cell>
                <cell><m>2</m></cell>
            </row>
        </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      Try the example below to evaluate the function at each input value.
    </p>

    <example>
      <title>Evaluating a Function from a Table</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Using the table, <xref ref="table-evaluate1"/>, evaluate the function at each input value.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <ul>
          <li><p><m>f(2)=0</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>f(9)=-6</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>f(-3)=9</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>f(40)=2</m></p></li>
        </ul>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <title>Evaluating a Function from a Table</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The amount of money, in dollars, that a child has in their piggy bank after <m>t</m> days is given by the function <m>P(t)</m>. The table below represents this function. Evaluate <m>P(2)</m> and interpret the result.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table-evaluate-bank-account">
          <title>Piggy Bank Balance</title>
          <tabular>
            <col right="minor"/>
            <col right="minor"/>
            <col right="minor"/>
            <col right="minor"/>
            <col/>
            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell><m>t</m></cell>
                <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                <cell><m>3</m></cell>
            </row>

            <row>
                <cell><m>P(t)</m></cell>
                <cell><m>5.5</m></cell>
                <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                <cell><m>7.84</m></cell>
                <cell><m>5</m></cell>
            </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>P(2)=7.84</m>. This means that after 2 days, the child has $<m>7.84</m> in their piggy bank.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

       <example xml:id="ex-function-values-table">
      <title>Reading a Function Table</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose <m>f(x)</m> is represented by the table below.</p>
        <table xml:id="table-function-values">
          <title>Function Values</title>
          <tabular>
            <row header="yes"><cell><m>x</m></cell><cell>0</cell><cell>2</cell><cell>5</cell><cell>8</cell><cell>10</cell></row>
            <row><cell><m>f(x)</m></cell><cell>12</cell><cell>15</cell><cell>18</cell><cell>21</cell><cell>25</cell></row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
        <ol marker="a.">
          <li><p>Find <m>f(5)</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Find <m>f(10)</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Find the value of <m>x</m> for which <m>f(x)=21</m>.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <ol marker="a.">
          <li><p>The input <m>5</m> corresponds to the output <m>18</m>, so <m>f(5)=18</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>The input <m>10</m> corresponds to the output <m>25</m>, so <m>f(10)=25</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>The output <m>21</m> occurs when the input is <m>8</m>, so <m>x=8</m>.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-water-consumption-table">
      <title>A Contextual Example</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Let <m>W=c(h)</m> represent the number of cups of water consumed after <m>h</m> hours of hiking.</p>
        <table xml:id="table-water-consumption">
          <title>Hiking Data</title>
          <tabular>
            <row header="yes"><cell><m>h</m></cell><cell>1</cell><cell>2</cell><cell>4</cell><cell>6</cell></row>
            <row><cell><m>c(h)</m></cell><cell>2</cell><cell>3</cell><cell>6</cell><cell>8</cell></row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
        <p>Evaluate <m>c(4)</m> and interpret the result.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>The input is <m>4</m> hours, and the corresponding output is <m>6</m> cups of water. Thus, <m>c(4)=6</m>, meaning that 6 cups of water have been consumed after 4 hours of hiking.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Evaluate-Graphs">
    <title>Evaluating With Graphs</title>
    <p>A graph represents many input-output pairs at once. To evaluate a function using a graph:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><p>Locate the input on the horizontal axis.</p></li>
      <li><p>Move vertically until you reach the graph.</p></li>
      <li><p>Read the corresponding output on the vertical axis.</p></li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      In both <xref ref="Eval-Graph-figure1"/> and <xref ref="Eval-Graph-figure2"/> we see the graph of the function <m>g(x)</m>. The graph in <xref ref="Eval-Graph-figure2"/> has additional lines to help evaluate the function at a specific point. 
    </p>


    <sidebyside>   
<figure xml:id="Eval-Graph-figure1">
     <image width="60%">
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="Eval-Graph-prefigure1">
        <title>Bubble Diagram</title>
        <description>Graph of Quadratic Function</description>
     <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="0">
  <definition>g(x)=-(x+1)^2+4</definition>
  <coordinates bbox="(-4,-3,4,5)">
    <grid-axes/>
    <graph function="g"/>
    <label anchor="(3,4)" alignment="center" clear-background="yes">
      <m>g(x)</m>
    </label>
  </coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure> 
 </image>
</figure>


<figure xml:id="Eval-Graph-figure2">
     <image width="60%">
    <caption>This is a Function</caption>
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="Eval-Graph-prefigure2">
        <title>Graph of Quadratic Function</title>
        <description>Graph of the quadratic function with lines to help evaluate.</description>
<diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="0">
  <definition>g(x)=-(x+1)^2+4</definition>
  <coordinates bbox="(-4,-3,4,5)">
    <grid-axes/>
    <graph function="g"/>
    <label anchor="(3,4)" alignment="center" clear-background="yes">
      <m>g(x)</m>
    </label>
    <line endpoints="((-2,-4),(-2,0))" stroke="red" infinite="yes"/>
    <line endpoints="((1,3),(-1,3))" stroke="green" infinite="yes"/>
  </coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure> 
 </image>
</figure>
    </sidebyside> 

    <p>
  To evaluate <m>g(3)</m>, locate <m>x=3</m> on the horizontal axis and move vertically to the graph (this is shown by the vertical red line). Find the point where the vertical line intersects the graph and see the corresponding output by following the horizontal green line back to the <m>y</m>-axis. The corresponding output is <m>5</m>. Therefore, <m>g(3)=5</m>.
</p>

    <example xml:id="example-evaluate-graph1">
      <title>Finding an Output from a Graph</title>
      <statement><p>Use the same graph,<xref ref="Eval-Graph-figure1"/>, to find the value of
      </p>
    <ol>
      <li><p><m>g(-3)</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>g(0)</m></p></li>
    </ol>
    </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p><m>g(-3)=0</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>g(0)=3</m></p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

        <example xml:id="example-evaluate-graph2">
      <title>Finding an Input from a Graph</title>
      <statement><p>Use the same graph,<xref ref="Eval-Graph-figure1"/>, to find the value of <m>x</m> for each of the following outputs:
      </p>
    <ol>
      <li><p><m>g(x)=4</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>g(x)=0</m></p></li>
    </ol>
    </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p><m>g(x)=4</m> when <m>x=-1</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>g(x)=0</m> when <m>x=-3</m> and when <m>x=1</m></p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Evaluate-Equations">
    <title>Evaluating With Equations</title>
    <p>
      Like graphs, equations typically have infinitely many input-output pairs. To evaluate a function given as an equations we replace the input variable with the number we choose for the input. It's helpful to begin evaluating an equation by first replacing the x's (or whatever the input variable name is) with a set of parenthesis and then placing the number into the parenthesis. This reduces the chance that you make an error in order of operations or placing a negative in the wrong location.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, to evaluate <m>f(x)=x^2-3x+5</m> at <m>x=4</m>, we first replace the <m>x</m> with parentheses and then place the number <m>4</m> into the parentheses:
    </p>
    <p>
  <md>
    <mrow>f(x) \amp =x^2 - 3x + 5</mrow>
    <mrow>f() \amp = ()^2 - 3() + 5</mrow>
    <mrow>f(4) \amp = (4)^2 - 3(4) + 5</mrow>
    <mrow>f(4) \amp = 16 - 12 + 5</mrow>
    <mrow>f(4) \amp = 9</mrow>
  </md>
  </p>

  <example xml:id="example-evaluate-equation1">
      <title>Evaluating a Function from an Equation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Given the function <m>f(x)=\frac{1}{2}x-4</m> find each of the following:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>The value <m>f(3)</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>The value of <m>x</m> when <m>f(x)=-2</m>.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <ol>
          <li><p>To find <m>f(3)</m>, we replace the <m>x</m> in the equation with parentheses and then place the number <m>3</m> into the parentheses:</p>
            <md>
              <mrow>f(x) \amp = \frac{1}{2}x - 4</mrow>
              <mrow>f() \amp = \frac{1}{2}() - 4</mrow>
              <mrow>f(3) \amp = \frac{1}{2}(3) - 4</mrow>
              <mrow>f(3) \amp = \frac{3}{2} - 4</mrow>
              <mrow>f(3) \amp = -\frac{5}{2}</mrow>
            </md>
          </li>
          <li><p>To find the value of <m>x</m> when <m>f(x)=-2</m>, we set the equation equal to -2 and solve for <m>x</m>.</p>
            <md>
              <mrow>-2 \amp = \frac{1}{2}x - 4</mrow>
              <mrow>-2 + 4 \amp = \frac{1}{2}x - 4 + 4</mrow>
              <mrow>2 \amp = \frac{1}{2}x</mrow>
              <mrow>2 * 2 \amp = x</mrow>
              <mrow>x \amp = 4</mrow>
            </md>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </solution>
    
  </example>
  </subsection>


